    {
      "id": "hbnuofm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Conditions for Coral Growth"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Corals require shallow water conditions (depth less than 50m) to allow sufficient sunlight penetration for photosynthesis.",
        "Optimal ocean temperature for tropical coral reef growth ranges strictly between 20°C and 21°C.",
        "Moderate salinity between 27‰ and 35‰ is mandatory; extreme freshwater or hypersaline conditions are harmful.",
        "Clear and sediment-free water is essential to prevent clogging of polyp feeding structures and ensure light transmission."
      ],
      "id": "dblu2kf"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "6ejrop5",
      "content": "• Temperature around 20–30°C"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "nrcuhxj",
      "content": "• Clear, shallow water"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "River estuaries and deltas do not support coral reefs due to high sediment accumulation and low salinity.",
        "Reef-building corals are primarily confined to tropical oceans between 30°N and 30°S latitudes.",
        "Symbiotic zooxanthellae algae provide food to coral polyps through photosynthesis in photic waters."
      ],
      "id": "bqfw35x"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "au2345k",
      "content": "• Low sedimentation"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "6om4o6c",
      "content": "• Salinity stability"
    }
  ],
